le to talk to him this time and  says i won't hurt you just tell me who  you are and there is no answer and all  of that starts to convince her that it's  coming from the vault she tells hugo  this he says that she's special and that  he has absolute faith in her  pharah starts to convince lewis to help  her by telling him that they are  technologically bankrupt ever since  their atmosphere was set on fire she  continues the story that they need the  navigation module because they have  ships but can't make their way to a  different system to rebuild their  civilization as salvor's watching the  troops outside the fence assemble a  weapon she thinks there's a larger  strategy at play here but she can't  figure it out the vault isn't an acronym  or alien at all she believes it's from  harry but she has this crisis of faith  that maybe lewis is right maybe her  being an outlier is the reason that  things are going wrong and maybe the  visions are warning her to stay away  hugo counters this by saying what if you  are seldon's plan which i guess you  can't blame him for not understanding  how this works and picking up on the  fact that there is something going on  with salvor we leave them and in the  closing moments we see the cryopod  holding gale moving through space and it  approaches what looks like a spacecraft  as it cuts to black and the episode ends  there so there's a couple of things  happening on trantor something's up with  the new brother dawn brother day is  starting to think of harry selden's  predictions again in light of the new  crises that are emerging and brother  dusk is trying to defend the decisions  he made 35 years ago it's interesting  but it feels like it's taking its time  getting wherever it's going the  religious angle is essentially brand new  so i'm not sure what to expect with day  heading to the conclave the terminus  side of things is mostly the same as  last week the enacrions are there  there's a split in how they should move  forward salvor is having visions and i  wish i could say it got more compelling  as we're watching it unfold but this  episode didn't feel like a big leap  forward the thing that's most  interesting here for me is what happened  to rach gail and harry and it all kind  of undercuts the current events salvor  brings it up in this episode when she's  arguing with louis that harry says the  foundation survives the first crisis and  that makes all the running around in  circles a lot less dramatic i felt a  sense of relief when brother dusk  ordered the guy to go to terminus  because i think salvor is probably  correct that that's what an acrion is  trying to do so let's get there there's  a problem baked into a story that's  built on psycho history how do you make  it exciting when someone already figured  out how the future is going to play in  the beginning azimoth jumped from one  crisis to the next until it got  predictable and then he found different  ways to mix things up there were  different scenarios that came up as the  new civilization was developing the tv  show seems content to take its time and  present a lot of mysteries along the way  but even though everything's connected  it doesn't always feel like it so it's  promising to see that we get a glimpse  of gail here at the end because maybe  understanding what's going on there will  add to what's happening on terminus give  it a feeling it's going somewhere the  ideas of belief and faith are all over  this episode you have the populace's  belief in the genetic dynasty you have  that being backed up by the religious  leaders the cleon's faith in their  purpose and the encyclopedist faith in  harry selden all of that is being called  into question and then you have salvor  she's on the opposite side of the people  on terminus she's not so sure how she  feels about just sitting back and  believing that harry's plan would have  figured this all out but then she's also  having this personal crisis of faith  that's getting in the way of her moving  to try to figure out what to do with the  enacrion hopefully next week we'll see  how that plays out and what that'll mean  for the rest of the season last week i  decided to hold off on doing another  book to show video because i thought  there would be a lot of overlap with  this week there's a moment in episode 4  where salvor uses the saying that the  character is best known for in the books  violence is the last refuge of the  incompetent it's both a nod to the  source material but also kind of a wtf  moment because it points out how  different the show's version is so i'll  get into that in my next video i'll  discuss the changes that have happened  in episodes three and four and what i  think it means for how the story is  going to play out because i do think  this is a good place to leave this one  so let me know in the comments what you  think's going on what you're hoping to  see please like this video if you  enjoyed it please subscribe to my  channel if you haven't already thanks  for watching i'll talk to you soon


